==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 12 NOTES — Wireshark Part 1
Date: 09-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- Wireshark is the GUI version of what tcpdump does: it captures packets on an interface and decodes them
- The window has three panes: packet list, packet details (layer by layer), packet bytes (hex + ASCII)
- Loopback (lo) carries traffic from my machine to itself; eth0 carries traffic on my real network
- A display filter hides packets AFTER capture (still in the file); a capture filter stops them being saved at all
- Display filter syntax is Wireshark's own (tcp.port == 8080), not the BPF syntax tcpdump uses
- tcp.flags.syn == 1 matches both SYN and SYN-ACK packets; adding && tcp.flags.ack == 0 leaves only the first SYN
- The packet details pane shows request/response fields directly (Host, User-Agent, Server, Content-Length, Time since request)
- Wireshark saves .pcapng; tcpdump can read it with -r

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] capture on Loopback: lo while curl-ing my local server
[ ] save the capture as .pcapng
[ ] display filters: tcp.port == 8080, http, http && tcp.port == 8080
[ ] display filters: tcp.flags.syn == 1 (with and without ack == 0)
[ ] expand the request and response packets in the details pane
[ ] capture on eth0, filter icmp || dns

